Approach 2 — Parent Pointer + HashMap
Idea

Instead of storing the complete root-to-node paths as lists, assign every node its parent.

For example:

                 3
               /   \
              5     1
             / \
            6   2
               / \
              7   4

Parent map:

5 → 3
1 → 3
6 → 5
2 → 5
7 → 2
4 → 2

Now:

Start at p.
Put all its ancestors into a HashSet.
Start at q.
Move upward until you meet a node in that set.

That node is the LCA.

Code

class Solution {

    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(
            TreeNode root,
            TreeNode p,
            TreeNode q) {

        Map<TreeNode, TreeNode> parent = new HashMap<>();

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();

        parent.put(root, null);
        queue.offer(root);

        // Build parent map
        while (!parent.containsKey(p)
                || !parent.containsKey(q)) {

            TreeNode curr = queue.poll();

            if (curr.left != null) {
                parent.put(curr.left, curr);
                queue.offer(curr.left);
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                parent.put(curr.right, curr);
                queue.offer(curr.right);
            }
        }

        // Store ancestors of p
        Set<TreeNode> ancestors = new HashSet<>();

        while (p != null) {
            ancestors.add(p);
            p = parent.get(p);
        }

        // Find first common ancestor of q
        while (!ancestors.contains(q)) {
            q = parent.get(q);
        }

        return q;
    }
}

