Approach 3 — Iterative DFS + Parent Map

The same idea can be implemented without BFS.

We only need to discover the parent of every node.

Code

class Solution {

    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(
            TreeNode root,
            TreeNode p,
            TreeNode q) {

        Map<TreeNode, TreeNode> parent = new HashMap<>();

        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();

        parent.put(root, null);
        stack.push(root);

        while (!parent.containsKey(p)
                || !parent.containsKey(q)) {

            TreeNode curr = stack.pop();

            if (curr.left != null) {
                parent.put(curr.left, curr);
                stack.push(curr.left);
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                parent.put(curr.right, curr);
                stack.push(curr.right);
            }
        }

        Set<TreeNode> ancestors = new HashSet<>();

        while (p != null) {
            ancestors.add(p);
            p = parent.get(p);
        }

        while (!ancestors.contains(q)) {
            q = parent.get(q);
        }

        return q;
    }
}

The complexity is still:

Time = O(n)
Space = O(n)

The difference is traversal strategy rather than asymptotic complexity.

Approach 4 — Recursive DFS: Optimal

Now we get to the most important solution.

This is the approach in your code.

Core Idea

At every node, ask:

Did I find p or q in my left subtree?
Did I find p or q in my right subtree?

There are three important situations.

Case 1 — Current node is null

Nothing found:

return null;
Case 2 — Current node is p or q

Immediately return it:

if (root == p || root == q) {
    return root;
}

Why?

Because if the other node is somewhere below it, this node will ultimately be the LCA.

Case 3 — Search both subtrees
left = search(left subtree)
right = search(right subtree)

Then:

Both non-null
left != null
right != null

This means:

one target is on the left
one target is on the right

Therefore current node is the LCA.

return root;
Only left is non-null

Then both targets are effectively found in the left side, so return:

return left;
Only right is non-null

Return:

return right;
Your Code — Optimal Solution

class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        
        if(root==null||root==p||root==q){
            return root;
        }

        TreeNode left=lowestCommonAncestor(root.left,p,q);
        TreeNode right=lowestCommonAncestor(root.right,p,q);

        if(left==null){
            return right;
        } else if(right==null){
            return left;
        } else{
            return root;
        }
    }
}

Your solution is the standard optimal solution for this problem.

